# Aleph Edge configuration
#
# These are PROCESS ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES. The app does not read a `.env`
# file: set them in the shell or service that starts Aleph Edge, e.g.
#   PowerShell:  $env:ALEPH_EDGE_BINANCE_FUTURES_BASE = "https://testnet.binancefuture.com"
#   bash:        export ALEPH_EDGE_BINANCE_FUTURES_BASE=https://testnet.binancefuture.com
# No host is embedded in the UI; it asks the Rust core for every endpoint.

# The Sentinel deployment to talk to.
# Default: https://ribqa.com
# ALEPH_EDGE_API_BASE=https://ribqa.com

# Phone relay.
#
# Leave it EMPTY normally: it is derived from the API base (https→wss,
# http→ws). Two knobs for one deployment is how a desk ends up authenticating
# against one host and streaming from another. Set it only when the relay
# really runs on another machine.
# ALEPH_EDGE_RELAY_URL=wss://example.test

# Where live futures orders go (Binance USDⓈ-M REST base).
#
# Default: https://fapi.binance.com (production). Point it at the Futures
# testnet for a dry run before real money; the top bar then shows TESTNET.
# This only moves WHERE orders go. Sending real orders at all needs a live
# build (`npm run tauri build -- --features live`) AND, per bot, switching
# "Real money" on in the app with the typed confirmation. The default build
# is simulation-only.
# ALEPH_EDGE_BINANCE_FUTURES_BASE=https://testnet.binancefuture.com

# Minutes of vault inactivity before it locks itself.
#
# Default: 30, clamped to 1–1440; an empty or invalid value falls back to the
# default. The timer resets whenever the vault is USED (unlock, reading,
# adding, removing or listing a key), not by how long it has been open: the
# engine reads the key on every tick while it manages a live position, so the
# vault stays open while money is at risk.
# ALEPH_EDGE_VAULT_IDLE_MINUTES=30
